Adenomyosis
Obstetrics Gynaecology & Womens health
Menorrhagia, Severe secondary dysmenorrhoea, Globally enlarged boggy uterus

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OGW-001
Reason for Visit: Severe, intractable pelvic pain and acute heavy menstrual bleeding causing orthostatic dizziness requiring immediate evaluation to prevent hemodynamic compromise.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated acute exacerbation of menorrhagia with new-onset dizziness and severe cramping unresponsive to over-the-counter analgesia. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to assess hemodynamic stability and manage acute pain to prevent deterioration and potential emergency department transfer before the next available in-hours appointment.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: The bleeding is heavier than ever, soaking through a pad every hour, and the cramps are so severe I cannot stand up.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms commenced progressively over the past six months, initially with dull pelvic pressure, but escalated sharply 12 hours ago with the onset of current menses. The patient reports heavy menstrual bleeding worsening over time, now passing large clots. This is accompanied by severe secondary dysmenorrhoea presenting as cramping pelvic pain that radiates to the lower back. The chronic, dragging sensation has acutely intensified, rendering her unable to mobilize effectively. Pre-menstrual spotting was noted for three days prior. She reports a history of painful intercourse but is currently not sexually active due to the pain.
Functional Status: Bedbound currently due to acute pain and dizziness on standing.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone, high risk of falls secondary to potential hypovolemia.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 105/65 mmHg | HR: 98 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 18 bpm | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Patient appears pale, diaphoretic, and in visible distress, guarding the lower abdomen.
Physical Exam: Abdominal examination reveals a globally enlarged, symmetrically boggy, and tender uterus palpable just above the pubic symphysis, consistent with a size not exceeding a 12-week pregnancy. Marked suprapubic tenderness without rebound or guarding. Moderate active vaginal bleeding noted on inspection of undergarments.
Environmental Observations: Clear pathways to the bathroom, adequately heated, accessible telephone.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against electronic health record. Patient taking ibuprofen 400mg every 6 hours with no relief; no other active medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Adenomyosis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Ectopic Pregnancy - Ruled out due to negative urine hCG test performed on site.

Pelvic Inflammatory Disease - Unlikely due to absence of fever, normal vaginal discharge prior to menses, and no cervical motion tenderness.

Endometrial Carcinoma - Unlikely given the symmetrically boggy uterus rather than an irregular mass, and age demographic.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute exacerbation of heavy menstrual bleeding and severe secondary dysmenorrhoea resulting in borderline tachycardia and orthostatic symptoms. The clinical objective evidence, including an actively bleeding, tender, and enlarged boggy uterus, combined with the failure of oral analgesia, confirms that the patient is at imminent risk of hypovolemia and unmanageable pain. This presentation satisfies MBS criteria for an urgent after-hours visit, as the assessment and initiation of hemostatic and analgesic interventions could not be safely delayed until the next in-hours period without risking severe clinical deterioration requiring ambulance transport.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered intramuscular ketorolac 30mg for acute pain relief. Prescribed oral tranexamic acid 1g three times daily during menses to reduce bleeding. Recommended formal pelvic ultrasound and full blood count tomorrow.
Positive Behavioral Change: To manage pelvic pain effectively, apply a heating pad to the lower abdomen for 20 minutes at a time when cramps begin; achieving immediate, tangible comfort will reinforce the habit of early, non-pharmacological pain intervention. To monitor blood loss, keep a daily tally of saturated pads; seeing the number decrease with the new medication will validate adherence to the tranexamic acid regimen.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not applicable.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not applicable.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 48 hours to check hemoglobin levels and review ultrasound results.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transport to the Emergency Department or call 000 if experiencing a fainting episode, soaking more than two maxi pads per hour for two consecutive hours, or if pain becomes unbearable despite the administered medications.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending formal ultrasound and laboratory investigations; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: Expect bleeding to decrease within 24 hours of starting tranexamic acid. Pain should improve significantly over the next 4 to 6 hours.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency care if you experience syncope, worsening shortness of breath, or bleeding that exceeds two pads per hour.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Tranexamic acid helps blood to clot in the uterus, directly reducing the volume of menstrual bleeding. The injection provided will quickly block the pain signals causing your severe cramps.
Actionable Steps: Start taking the tranexamic acid immediately as prescribed and use the heating pad for early pain control.

